 Labels use a middle baseline. Null or undefined text skips the row; the default
 for a null datum is an empty string. Invalid x/y values also skip the row.
 
+### Halo
+
+`halo` keeps labels legible over busy marks, such as values inside heatmap
+cells or names inside pie slices, without measuring text for a background box.
+Each label gets a stroked copy painted directly beneath it, so the halo follows
+the glyph outlines in SVG and Canvas alike.
+
+```ts
+text(cells, {
+  x: 'column',
+  y: 'row',
+  text: 'value',
+  halo: { stroke: 'white', strokeWidth: 3 },
+})
+```
+
+| Option          | Type     | Default                             | Meaning                     |
+| --------------- | -------- | ----------------------------------- | --------------------------- |
+| `stroke`        | `string` | `var(--ts-chart-text-halo, Canvas)` | Halo paint                  |
+| `strokeOpacity` | `number` | Renderer default                    | Halo stroke opacity         |
+| `strokeWidth`   | `number` | `3`                                 | Halo width; `0` disables it |
+
+`halo: true` uses the defaults. The halo uses round joins, reuses the label's
+position, rotation, and typography, and receives the same `states` styles as
+its label. A zero width or zero opacity paints no halo.
+
 The interaction point is at the offset label origin. Its semantic values remain
 the original x/y channels. Without an explicit key, `text` tries a unique
 top-level or nested `data.id`, then x, y, and the x/y tuple. Supply `key` when
